---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const CG = "codegen/src/iki_codegen/models.py";
const N = "spec/src/iki_spec/naming.py";
const SYNC = "server/src/iki_server/plugins/sync.py";

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "A type is a table, with its history beside it" },
  { id: "how", label: "What the build writes, and what the deploy adds" },
  { id: "rules", label: "The details" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="A type is a table, with its history beside it">
    <p>
      A plugin declares a type in a YAML manifest under its <code>types/</code>. Iki's code
      generator turns it into Django models: one table per type, with a typed column per field,
      extending <code>iki_entity</code>; an append-only history table beside it; a table per list
      of references, files, people or rows; and, for a type with an identity, a table of its
      registry entries. At deploy time <code>sync_plugins</code> puts the triggers on those tables
      that hold uniqueness, references, rules and locks in the database (<Adr n={[7, 32, 33]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="What the build writes, and what the deploy adds">
    <p>
      <Path p={CG} sym="def render_models(" /> renders a plugin's <code>models.py</code>. Its
      output is deterministic: the same plugin always renders the same bytes. Each type gets:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Its table</strong>, named <code>&lt;app&gt;_&lt;type&gt;</code> (<Path
          p={N}
          sym="def type_table("
        />), extending <Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/models.py" sym="class Entity(" /> or
        its parent type by multi-table inheritance, with named CHECK constraints for each rule the
        database can enforce (<Path p={N} sym="def constraint_name(" />).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Its history table</strong>, <code>&lt;table&gt;__history</code>, keyed on (entity,
        revision), with every field the type ever had, removed ones included, in wide column
        types, and no constraints or foreign keys: references, files and people are bare UUIDs, so
        history survives whatever happens to what it pointed to. <PageLink to="Revisions and history" /> covers how it's written and read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A table per list</strong> of references, files, people or rows (<code
          >&lt;table&gt;__&lt;field&gt;</code
        >), keyed on (entity, position), so each item has a real foreign key; a list of rows has a
        column per row field (<Adr n="0137" />). Other lists are arrays.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For a type with an identity</strong>, a table of its entries (<code
          >&lt;table&gt;__entry</code
        >) with the identity fields and those marked <code>registry</code>. The type's own table
        holds each project's record (<Adr n={[115, 165]} />). See <PageLink to="Identity and HRIDs" />.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An extension, one plugin's fields on another's type, gets the same tables, keyed by the
      extended type's <code>entity_id</code>. A <code>person</code> field is a foreign key to <code
        >iki_user</code
      >, which never loses a row: people are deactivated, never deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Descriptions and units aren't in the models. <Path p={SYNC} /> writes them as table and column
      comments at deploy time, so changing them needs no migration. It also installs, on the tables
      that need them, the triggers each of these pages covers:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>HRID sequences, one per prefix: <PageLink to="Identity and HRIDs" hash="hrids" />.</li>
      <li>Unique fields and the <code>unique</code> and <code>no_overlap</code> rules: <PageLink to="Saving and validation" hash="database" />.</li>
      <li>References' graph edges and references' types: <PageLink to="Links between records" />.</li>
      <li>Locked records' guards: <PageLink to="Locks and corrections" />.</li>
      <li>A request's sides, each writing only its own fields: <PageLink to="Asking and serving" />.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <code>migrate_deployment</code> runs it as the migrator, in the transaction that runs the
      plugins' migrations, so nothing writes to a table whose triggers haven't caught up (<Adr
        n="0134"
      />). How migrations are made and versioned is on <PageLink to="Migrations and versions" />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="The details">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Read-only views for analysts" open>
        <p>
          The sync also writes one read-only view per type in the schema <code>iki_views</code>{" "}
          (<code>iki_views.protein_protein</code>), joining the core row, the type's table, its
          parents', every installed extension and, for an identity, its entry's values. Lists of
          references or files appear as arrays. The views use <code>security_invoker</code>. If{" "}
          <code>IKI_DB_ANALYST_ROLE</code> names a role, it gets read access to the views and the
          tables behind them, current values and history, and nothing else (<Path
            p={SYNC}
            sym="def _analyst_grants("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Core types">
        <p>
          The types every deployment has are declared the same way, in <Path
            p="spec/src/iki_spec/core/types"
          />, and built into the app <code>iki_plugin_iki__core</code>. The <PageLink
            to="Core types"
          /> page lists them.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0038" /> names <code>iki_plugin_core</code>; the package is <code>iki_plugin_iki__core</code>.</li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Files and decisions">
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={CG} /></li>
          <li><Path p={N} /></li>
          <li><Path p={SYNC} /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0007" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0032" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0033" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0134" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0137" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
